Build a Custom VPC with Subnets via CLI
Learning Objectives
By the end of this lab, you will be able to:

Deploy a VPC with custom CIDR blocks using AWS CLI on LocalStack
Configure public and private subnets within a VPC
Attach an internet gateway and configure route tables for internet access
Verify network topology using AWS CLI describe commands
Prerequisites
Basic understanding of networking concepts (CIDR notation, IP addressing)
Familiarity with AWS VPC components (subnets, route tables, gateways)
Comfort using a Linux terminal
Basic AWS CLI command structure knowledge
Environment Setup
Al Nafi provides a single Linux machine via Start Lab. Use it for all steps below.

Step 1: Install Docker and LocalStack
sudo apt update && sudo apt install -y docker.io python3-pip
sudo systemctl start docker
pip3 install localstack awscli-local
Step 2: Start LocalStack with VPC/EC2 services
# EC2 service covers VPC, subnets, route tables, and gateways
localstack start -d
Wait ~10 seconds, then verify it's running:

localstack status services
Confirm ec2 shows status running.

Step 3: Configure AWS CLI for LocalStack
aws configure set aws_access_key_id test
aws configure set aws_secret_access_key test
aws configure set region us-east-1
Use awslocal (wraps aws --endpoint-url=http://localhost:4566) for all commands below, or manually append --endpoint-url=http://localhost:4566 to each AWS CLI call.

Tasks
Task 1: Create the VPC
Create a VPC with CIDR block 10.0.0.0/16.

awslocal ec2 create-vpc --cidr-block 10.0.0.0/16
TODO: Capture the VpcId from the output JSON and export it as an environment variable:
